On Friday, February 1, 2013 7:10:04 PM UTC, Bob Carpenter wrote:
>
> So I'd suggest adding the bits of the model back in one
> statement at a time to isolate which statement is causing
> the NaN value.
>

It's when I include
beta1 ~ multi_normal(zeros, Sigma);
that it falls apart.

One thing I don't understand is this:

If I run this in the model block:

alpha1 ~ normal(0.0, 100.0);
print("alpha1=", alpha1);
tau ~ gamma(0.5, 0.0005);
print("tau=", tau, "; sigma_sq=", sigma_sq);

Sigma <- sigma_sq * inverse(D - p * A);

print(Sigma);
then it runs and prints alpha1, tau/sigma, and Sigma.

But once I add in beta1 like so:

alpha1 ~ normal(0.0, 100.0);
print("alpha1=", alpha1);
tau ~ gamma(0.5, 0.0005);
print("tau=", tau, "; sigma_sq=", sigma_sq);

Sigma <- sigma_sq * inverse(D - p * A);

print(Sigma);
beta1 ~ multi_normal(zeros, Sigma);
then it only prints alpha1. And the error is again related to the gamma_log for tau.

How is it that tau ~ gamma on its own is ok, but once the beta1 ~ multi_normal is added in it no longer works (even for what I believe would be the initial iteration)? I thought the model block was executed sequentially, but I guess I'm misunderstanding that aspect.

