Aha. Somehow I had it in my head from a very early stage that even if X is declared as an array of reals I can get a vector that spans the entire final index by specifying all but the final index value.  That belief was reinforced by this statement from your first reply on this thread:

====

> Also, if I am working with X[n,m,p], can I use statements like
> X[n,m] ~ student_t(4,z,sigma)
> where z is a vector of length p? That is, is X[i,j] the vector obtained by evaluating X[i,j,k] for k from 1:p?

Yes, as long as X is declared as

   real X[n,m,p];

or

   vector[p] X[n,m];

====

This led me to believe "OK, I get it, either of these is equivalent just like I thought."

Now I definitely understand why what I was trying does not work...but now I don't understand why what you said above _does_ work! But that's OK. Baby steps. For now I am up and running.

