Date: Mon, 11 Feb 2013 23:19:59 +0000
Subject: Re: [stan-users] stan memory usage increasing faster than expected
Message-ID: <EbCjxm9EPp8.TyB8Q-uqAmAJ@stan-users.groups.google.com>
In-Reply-To: <EbCjxm9EPp8._gmIFIhhuksJ@stan-users.groups.google.com>
References: <EbCjxm9EPp8._gmIFIhhuksJ@stan-users.groups.google.com>
Archived-At: <https://groups.google.com/g/stan-users/c/EbCjxm9EPp8/m/TyB8Q-uqAmAJ>
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset="utf-8"
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Hi Daniel,

Thanks for taking the time to do some profiling on Stan. I'm going to try to keep the response short.

The underlying algorithm for Stan is the No-U-Turn sampler (NUTS), which is an extension to Hamiltonian Monte Carlo (HMC). In order to use HMC, we need the gradient of the log probability function. That's what's being built in the Stan model. Stan is using (reverse-mode) automatic differentiation to calculate the gradients. It's the auto-diff that will scale memory out non-linearly.

The next reasonable question might be "is there a bound on memory usage?" and the short answer is no. Memory usage depends on the model you're building, the operations used on the parameters / derived parameters, vectorization, partially evaluated gradients, ...

When you hear about us talking about vectorized distributions, we're tackling two problems. The first is a consistency in the language so Stan is easier to use. The second problem we're addressing is partially evaluating gradients so we're not relying on auto-diff for the derivatives of known functions, such as pdfs of distributions. This reduces the memory footprint and increases speed.

A lot of this is covered in the preface of the manual. Hopefully that clears up some confusion.

Daniel

On Mon, Feb 11, 2013 at 2:34 AM, Daniel Jones <lawson...@gmail.com> wrote:

> Hi,
> I'm seeing some strange behavior with stan's memory usage. I have a stan model whose memory usage should increase linearly with the number of data points. However, I find that memory usage seems to increase as N^1.8. (To determine the exponent I generated simulated datasets of varying sizes and monitored stan's memory usage.)
>
> I have triple-checked the .stan file for my model and none of the vectors or matrices should increase in size faster than O(N). I'm wondering if perhaps I've missed something, or perhaps stan is doing something behind the scenes that I don't know about that's causing memory usage to increase nonlinearly.
>
> At any rate, I'm attaching the .stan file that I'm using. I'm running stan from the Linux command line.
>
> Thanks in advance,
> Daniel

