On 2/21/13 11:01 PM, rubi wrote:
> Thanks for the help.
> I set:
> parameters {
>    real<lower=0,upper=1> mu;
>    real<lower=0> sigmaS;
>    real<lower=0> c;
>    real<lower=0> d;
> }
> transformed parameters {
>    real<lower=0> a;
>    real<lower=0> b;
>    a <- -1*mu*(sigmaS + mu*mu - mu)/sigmaS;
>    b <- (sigmaS + mu*mu - mu)*(mu - 1)/sigmaS;
> }
>   However now I'm getting this error:
> Error : Invalid value of a: Error in function validate transformed params N4stan5agrad3varE: a is -0.4640491230132906:0,
> but must be greater than 0
>
> I'm puzzled as to how negative parameter values are sampled where all my limits are set to positive and all the
> distributions only have positive supports.
>
> Any idea?
>
> Thanks again,
> rubi

Stan requires all transformed parameters to satisfy
their declared constraints at the end of the transformed
parameter statements.

Your transformed parameter a is declared to be positive.
But its definition will produce a negative result whenever

   sigmaS > mu - mu*mu.

If you need to keep parameter a positive, then declare sigmaS to
satisfy the necessary constraint with

    real<lower=0, upper=(mu - mu * mu)> sigmaS;

Stan's constraint language is limited by the fact that the
constraints have to be declared in order.  But that'll work
here.

Better yet, find an alternative simpler way to parameterize
the model.

- Bob

