Content-Type: text/plain; charset="utf-8"
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Thanks a lot for the help.
I followed your advice and set the constraint on sigmaS and deleted the sampling from beta(1,1). I also changed sigmaS to be sampled from beta instead of from gamma, because sigmaS is the variance of beta distributed observations. (See code below)

As Bob's response alluded to perhaps the reparameterization is not ideal, because now I'm getting this error:
Initialization failed after 100 attempts.  Try specifying initial values, reducing ranges of constrained values, or reparameterizing the model.

The thing is that my observations are rates (i.e., 0 <= O[g][s][k] <= 1 for every k,s,g) so sampling them from beta makes sense, and my aim is to model their variance across subgroups and groups as I believe it is shared across these levels of the hierarchy. That's why my solution was to reparametrize the the variance of the beta distribution as a function of a and b and put a prior on that variance. If you easily see a straight forward alternative solution I'd be really grateful to hear about it.

Thanks again,
rubi

Code

data {
  int<lower=1> G; // number of groups
  int<lower=1> S; // number of subgroups
  int<lower=1> K; // number of observations in each subgroup
  matrix<lower=0,upper=1>[S,K] O[G];
}
parameters {

  real<lower=0,upper=1> mu;
  real<lower=0,upper=(mu - mu * mu)> sigmaS;

  real<lower=0> c;
  real<lower=0> d;
}
transformed parameters {
  real<lower=0> a;

  real<lower=0> b;

  a <- -1*mu*(sigmaS + mu*mu - mu)/sigmaS;
  b <- (sigmaS + mu*mu - mu)*(mu - 1)/sigmaS;
}
model {
   for (g in 1:G){
c ~ exponential(1);
d ~ exponential(1);
for (s in 1:S){

sigmaS ~ beta(c,d);

for (k in 1:K){
O[g,s,k] ~ beta(a,b);
}
}
  }
}

On Friday, February 22, 2013 2:44:43 AM UTC-5, Ben Goodrich wrote:
> On Thursday, February 21, 2013 6:29:52 PM UTC-5, rubi wrote:
> > Hi (again),
> > I have a the following hierarchical model with parameter transformations:
> > At the bottom of the hierarchy I have:
> > O[g][s][k] ~ Beta(a,b) - that is, Observation k from subgroup s of group g follows a beta distribution.
> > I reparametrize the a and b parameters in terms of the mean (mu) and variance (sigmaS) of the beta distribution:
> > a = -mu(sigmaS + mu^2 -mu)/sigmaS
> > b = (sigmaS + mu^2 -mu)(mu - 1)/sigmaS
> >
> > The next level up in hierarchy thus has priors for mu and sigmaS:
> > mu ~ Beta(1,1)
> > sigmaS ~ Gamma(c,d)
>
> What Bob said will fix your problems with the parameter support for sigmaS. Another thing is that if you are going to use a uniform prior on mu, that is fine but you should just comment out the
>
> mu ~ beta(1,1);
>
> line. It won't make a huge difference in terms of speed, but there is some overhead in evaluating that constant density, which doesn't affect the shape of the posterior distribution. In the absence of an explicitly specified prior, parameters are uniformally distributed over their support.
>
> Ben
>

