Thanks again for help.

I hope I understood Ben's suggestion correctly. My model is now:

model {
   for (g in 1:G){
c ~ exponential(1);
d ~ exponential(1);
for (s in 1:S){

lp__ <- lp__ - log1m(1-pow((1-pow(mu - mu * mu,c)),d)); //where 1-pow((1-pow(mu - mu * mu,c)),d) is the Kumaraswamy(c,d) CDF evaluated at mu  - mu*mu, which is supposed to correct lp for no mass in the interval [mu - mu * mu,1]

for (k in 1:K){
O[g,s,k] ~ beta(a,b);
}
}
  }
}

I'm still getting this error though:

Initialization failed after 100 attempts.  Try specifying initial values, reducing ranges of constrained values, or reparameterizing the model.

error occurred during calling the sampler; sampling not done

But I think that this is due to the fact the in O[g,s,k] ~ beta(a,b), the beta(a,b) is truncated (as beta(c,d) is for sigmaS ~ beta(c,d)).
Would it make sense to perform the same correction for O[g,s,k] ~ beta(a,b) (i.e., lp__ <- lp__ - log1m(CDF); where in this case CDF is the Kumaraswamy CDF evaluated at at some point which I should be close to the truncation point (say mu+3*sqrt(sigmaS))?

Thanks again,
rubi

