Sure.
Attached is the model and a toy data set.
I create a list (called O) of size G (# of groups) of SxK matrices (S is # subgroups and K is the # of observations in each subgroup) and call rstan with:
stan(file=model.stan,data=dat,iter=1000,chains=4)
where dat = list(G=G,S=S,K=K,O=O)
The model is:

At the bottom of the hierarchy I have:
O[g][s][k] ~ Beta(a,b) - that is, Observation k from subgroup s of group g follows a beta distribution.
I reparametrize the a and b parameters in terms of the mean (mu) and variance (sigmaS) of the beta distribution:
a = -mu(sigmaS + mu^2 -mu)/sigmaS
b = (sigmaS + mu^2 -mu)(mu - 1)/sigmaS

The next level up in hierarchy models subgroup variance and thus has a prior sigmaS and a flat prior for mu:

mu ~ Beta(1,1)
sigmaS ~ Gamma(c,d)

The next level up in the hierarchy (the top one) models the group level with c and d:

c ~ Exponential(1)
d ~ Exponential(1)

Since I have G groups I thought I need sample c and d inside the loop over g. Am I wrong?

Thanks,
rubi

