The answer is yes, but it's a pain. You'll have to override
grad_log_prob() in the c++ code that Stan generates to implement your
model, which requires a certain amount of familiarity with how Stan is
implemented.

Matt

On Sun, Feb 24, 2013 at 5:59 AM, Ben Goodrich <goodri...@gmail.com> wrote:
> On Sunday, February 24, 2013 7:09:55 AM UTC-5, ZiYuan Lin wrote:
>>
>> Hi, my log posterior is quite complex and I am not sure Stan can
>> efficiently compute it. So I have already calculated it by hand, can I
>> somehow pass it to Stan?
>
>
> No. With some effort, you could customize the generated C++ file to use an
> analytic gradient. Or Radford Neal's GRIMS package can do HMC in R with a
> function that computes the gradient analytically.
>
> However, the auto-differentiation mechanism in Stan is pretty clever and
> only contributes a constant multiple to the complexity of evaluating the
> log-posterior.
>
> Ben
