Content-Type: text/plain; charset="utf-8"
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

This has come up a few times, but I'm afraid we
don't have a good way to do that.  All of the
variables in an array or vector or matrix get the
same constraint.

You could define the transforms yourself if you add
the log Jacobian determinant.  See the chapter of the manual
on how we do it internally.

- Bob

On 2/26/13 6:25 PM, rubi wrote:
> A related question (from my model which appears in other posts):
> In my parameters I have:
> matrix<lower=0,upper=1>[G,S] mu;
> and
> matrix[G,S] sigmaS;
> where I want each sigmaS[g][s] element to be constrained by: <lower=0,upper=mu[g][s]-(mu[g][s]*mu[g][s]>
>
> Obviously, this statement:
> matrix<lower=0,upper=mu - (mu .* mu)>[G,S] sigmaS;
> won't compile
>
> Thanks a lot
>
> On Tuesday, January 29, 2013 6:55:18 PM UTC-5, cmg82 wrote:
>
>     Thanks Bob!!
>

>     El martes, 29 de enero de 2013 18:17:07 UTC-5, Bob Carpenter escribi�:

>
>         On 1/29/13 1:53 PM, cmg82 wrote:
>          > Suppose that under Parameters, I want to define a vector of parameters (for example, the betas in a linear
>         regression).
>          > If I know that one (and only one) of the elements of the vector is going to have for sure a support [0,1]
>         (for example),
>          > is it possible to constrain only that element of the vector?
>
>         Not directly, but you can do it indirectly.  Suppose
>         you want a total of N and the first is constrained.
>
>         parameters {
>             real<lower=0,upper=1> x1;
>             real x2_N[N-1];
>         }
>         transformed parameters {
>             real x[N];
>             x[1] <- x1;
>             for (n in 1:(N-1))
>               x[n+1] <- x2_N[n];
>         }
>         ... now use x ...
>
>         - Bob
>

